			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2218108">
									

<p>The weird text and bizarre language juxtaposed with the banal imagery is a neat device, however it's one that I think was carried off better in <a href="/scp-2557">SCP-2557</a>. In this particular case, what the article is at heart is a garden variety compulsion effect.</p>
<p>As with almost all articles that rely on a compulsion effect, I can't help but feel like the reader is &quot;on rails,&quot; so to speak. I am not drawn to any conclusions about what a phenomenon means because the writer carefully considered the salient qualities of the object and what kind of reactions they might engender. Instead I am shown that this thing will affect everyone that comes across it immediately and in the exact same way, and the dynamic becomes something weaker. It's not &quot;this thing is unsettling/dangerous/scary because it is X,&quot; but rather it's &quot;this thing is unsettling/dangerous/scary because I the author have directly declared that it is unsettling/dangerous/scary.&quot;</p>
<p>This is a general, long-running issue of mine, so it's not a feature unique to your writing or anything. But I was definitely hoping for some kind of different direction from the initial prompt than the one you took.</p>

